:: StoryTitle
The Martyrdom of Carpus, Papylus, and Agathonice


:: StoryData
{
  "ifid": "E8533CA6-DF1A-43AC-BD7E-26A57C2967D6",
  "format": "Harlowe",
  "format-version": "3.3.9",
  "start": "The Martyrdom of Papylus, Carpus and Agathonice",
  "zoom": 1
}


:: The Martyrdom of Papylus, Carpus and Agathonice {"position":"950,250","size":"100,100"}
(align:"==><==")[''The Martyrdom of Papylus, Carpus, and Agathonice'']
<div class="scene" data-scene="pergamum" data-caption="Pergamum, in the province of Asia" data-mono="acropolis"></div>
Please pick the tradition of the account:
[[Original Greek and Latin]] or [[Latin Recension (WIP)]]

<div class="endings-list"></div>


:: Original Greek and Latin {"position":"700,400","size":"100,100"}
During the reign of Marcus Aurelius,<span class="fn" data-src="eusebius" data-loc="4.15.48">Eusebius names Carpus, Papylus, and Agathonice among the martyrs of Pergamum in his account of this period.</span> the christians [[Papylus]], [[Carpus]], and [[Agathonice]] were Martyred.<span class="tradition" data-tradition="original"></span>

(align:"===><===")[Please choose a Saint to follow in the footsteps of.
]
<div class="icon-frame"><img class="icon-img" src="https://images.oca.org/icons/lg/october/1013carpuspapylusagathoodrus.jpg" alt="Icon of the martyrs Carpus, Papylus, Agathodorus, and Agathonice"><div class="icon-credit">Icon courtesy of the Orthodox Church in America<span class="fn" data-src="oca"></span></div></div>


:: Papylus {"position":"500,600","size":"100,100"}
<div class="scene" data-scene="tribunal" data-caption="Before the proconsul's tribunal" data-mono="choice" data-mono-caption="Will you sacrifice?"></div>
Having been brought before the proconsul, you watch as your fellow Christian, Carpus, stands strong before his quesitoning, and is brought up to be scraped and tortured after refusing to sacrifice. You have no choice but to watch as he is tortured until his voice goes hoarse before you.

The Proconsul turns to you now, and after a series of questions, he asks, "Will you sacrifice?"

Choose: [[Sacrifice]] or [[Refuse]]


:: Sacrifice {"position":"425,800","size":"100,100"}
<div class="scene" data-scene="altar" data-caption="Incense before the image of the emperor" data-mono="offering"></div>
Intimidated by the torture of your fellow Christian, you relent and sacrifice to the Emperor.

You survive, but are now apostate and an outsider to the church in which you were born. The community which you once called your children refuses to associate with you. Their trust in themselves and their community has been damaged. For if their teacher can not even stand strong beside his brothers, how could they?

<div class="ending" data-n="1" data-title="The Apostate" data-tradition="original"></div>


:: Refuse {"position":"575,800","size":"100,100"}
<div class="scene" data-scene="pyre" data-caption="The pyres of Pergamum" data-mono="stake" data-mono-caption="Nailed to the stake"></div>
Refusing to sacrifice, you too are tortured and scraped before walking proud to your building pyre.

Nailed and raised up within the flames, you are the first to die, giving up your soul to God in peace.

Unbeknowst to you, Carpus shares a few last words with the crowd before being put to the flame himself. Moments later, a woman breaks from the crowd and  jumps into the building flames, now standing by your side as you walk along the path towards Heaven's gates.

<div class="ending" data-n="2" data-title="The Fellow Martyr" data-true="yes" data-tradition="original"></div>


:: Carpus {"position":"700,600","size":"100,100"}
<div class="scene" data-scene="tribunal" data-caption="Before the proconsul's tribunal" data-mono="procession" data-mono-caption="Led in chains to the proconsul"></div>
As you and Papylus are brought forth to the Proconsul, you come to realization that you will be interrogated first. You have a sense of what they will ask, having been raised on the passions of martyrs prior.

The words of prior martyrs when facing their deaths run through your mind, and you must decide if you will follow in their footsteps. What is your own response?

[[Curse the Romans]] or [[Give a Speech (WIP)]]


:: Curse the Romans {"position":"650,700","size":"100,100"}
<div class="scene" data-scene="defiance" data-caption="Carpus curses his captors" data-mono="defiant"></div>
The Proconsul asks, "What is your name?"

"My name matters not! Send me to my death already, so that I might not see you, our opressors, any longer."

You stubbornly refuse to play their game, not explaining your religion, nor your views. You fight them at every step, and remain steadfast through torture not through strength drawn from faith, but your own will power.

The pagans in the crowd admire your grit, even if you blaspheme their gods, but the Christians there are not inspired. You don't go to your death like a martyr, but a common criminal. Papylus goes quietly afterward like a whisper after your flash.

A mother in the crowd, Agathonice, turns away to go care for her son at home. She never gets her own chance to be a martyr.

<div class="ending" data-n="5" data-title="The Common Thug" data-tradition="original"></div>


:: Give a Speech (WIP) {"position":"750,700","size":"100,100"}



:: Agathonice {"position":"900,600","size":"100,100"}
<div class="scene" data-scene="market" data-caption="The market of Pergamum" data-mono="market"></div>
While walking through the markets of Pergamus, you witness a growing crowd. Hearing the chatter of the crowd, you make your way towards the front and are shocked to see two of your fellow Christians, Papylus and Carpus, being charged.

The words of Carpus fill you with inspiration, and you start making your way towards their pyres, "This dinner hath been prepared for me; of this glorious dinner therefore I must needs partake and eat."<span class="fn" data-src="musurillo" data-loc="Greek recension, section/page TBD">Agathonice's own words in the Greek account. Author's to-do: name the translation this wording comes from.</span>

People cry out to you, saying, "Have pity on your son!"<span class="fn" data-src="musurillo" data-loc="Greek recension, section/page TBD"></span>

How do you respond?

[[Stand by your decision]] or [[Be convinced]]


:: Stand by your decision {"position":"825,800","size":"100,100"}
<div class="scene" data-scene="glory" data-caption="Lord, help me, for I flee to thee" data-mono="leap"></div>
You respond to their pleas, "He hath God who can have pity on him, for he is the protector of all..."

Shedding your outer garments, you leap on to one of the blessed martyr's pyres, joining them in their witness. Shouting out you say, "Lord, Lord, Lord help me, for I flee onto thee."

<div class="ending" data-n="3" data-title="The Bystander" data-true="yes" data-tradition="original"></div>


:: Be convinced {"position":"975,800","size":"100,100"}
<div class="scene" data-scene="pyre" data-caption="The smoke rises over Pergamum" data-mono="vigil"></div>
You look between both the pyres and the crowd, and allow doubt to creep in. Has this feast really been prepared for me? Or am I just trying to eat off another's plate? Who would care for my son in this life if not me?

You stand conflicted long enough for members of the crowd to grab your arms and clothes and pull you back away from the flames. You stare at the fire as the rising smoke carries the  souls of the two martyrs away to the heavens. Had you missed your chance?

<div class="ending" data-n="4" data-title="The Mother" data-tradition="original"></div>


:: Latin Recension (WIP) {"position":"1200,400","size":"100,100"}
<span class="tradition" data-tradition="recension"></span>


